gates as mean + CI. A seed table records all SEED values used.\n  - Scaling check: run the central parameter setting on N in {2e4, 5e4, 1e5, 2e5} (down-sampled versions of the main dataset) to report wall-clock runtime per Docta call (or, if the engine is opaque, a documented timing stub) and to confirm recovery is stable (within seeded CI) as N grows \u2014 protecting against the claim that rarity is only recoverable at small N or that support thresholds behave differently at scale.\n\n=========================================================================\n6. ANALYSIS PLAN AND FEASIBILITY\n=========================================================================\nPlanned analyses/tables/figures (to be produced in the write-up):\n  T1: Dataset summary table (support levels, lifts, noise levels).\n  T2: Main results \u2014 per (dataset, tau, grid cell): Rec, Prec, rank_true, FP_rate with 95% CIs; best settings per tau.\n  T3: Negative-control calibration table \u2014 FP_rate(tau) from Dataset_N across the grid.\n  T4: Ablation table (Dataset_A results by removed pattern).\n  T5: Difficulty-gradient table (Dataset_V0/V10/V30) \u2014 recovery frontier.\n  F1: Recovery vs FP_rate operating curve (each point a grid cell), with tau-iso-lines, to pick the operating point.\n  F2: rank_true boxplot across seeds by tau.\n  F3: runtime vs N (scaling check).\n\nCalibration recipe for REAL unlabeled tabular data (no ground truth) \u2014 derived from T1/T3/F1: (1) run the grid on the real dataset; (2) compute FP_rate(tau) analytically from the negative-control table as an upper bound for the null; (3) select the largest tau at which Rec in synthetic evaluation remains high while FP_rate stays <= 5%; (4) apply that tau + quality measure to the real data; (5) report patterns with their support/lift/p-values, requiring p <= alpha (FDR-corrected) and lift >= lift_min, so the output consists only of patterns passing the Section-3 criteria; (6) confirm stability by re-running with two seeds and retaining patterns present in both. This makes the method usable without labels while the credibility of threshold choice comes from the controlled synthetic evaluation.\n\nFeasibility constraints (explicit): current environment is Python 3.14.4 with NO pandas/numpy/sklearn and no mining libraries; network is disabled. Any future execution therefore has stated PRECONDITIONS: (i) install Docta (or an equivalent interface shim implementing the Section-1 contract) and baseline libraries (numpy/pandas/scipy; mlxtend or pysubgroup for baseline itemset/subgroup enumeration) via allowed package channels in an enabled environment; (ii) if installation is impossible, use minimal pure-Python fallbacks for BASELINE comparisons only (a simple frequent-itemset enumeration by support threshold over discretized columns, and a 2x2 lift/chi-square check per cell) \u2014 these fallbacks never substitute for Docta, they only bound what an engine-agnostic baseline achieves. The design itself does not depend on any specific package and no execution has been or is claimed to have been performed.\n\n=========================================================================\n7. ASSUMPTIONS vs FIXED REQUIREMENTS\n=========================================================================\nASSUMED (by design, due to lack of source materials \u2014 flagged as such in the write-up):\n  - Docta's identity/capabilities/API: an automated pattern-discovery/AutoML-style engine for tabular data exposing the Section-1 contract; exact parameter names/output format unknown and handled via thin adapters.\n  - \"Rare\" is defined dual: low support (tau in [0.005,0.02]) AND statistical interestingness (lift/chi-square/binomial p with FDR; WRAcc/relative-risk in supervised mode) \u2014 per Section 3.\n  - Evaluation uses a purpose-built synthetic dataset with planted ground truth (no public dataset available; network disabled), plus a noise-only negative control.\n  - Synthetic schema/composition (Section 2) reflects a \"typical\" mixed tabular dataset; specific column names and pattern set are illustrative-but-fixed for reproducibility.\n  - Feasibility preconditions (installation of Docta/shim and baseline libs, or pure-Python fallback bounds) may be specified for future execution.\n\nFIXED REQUIREMENTS (from the brief \u2014 non-negotiable):\n  - Deliverable is a DESIGN ONLY: rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility; explicitly DO NOT claim an experiment was run. (Complied: this document contains no executed results.)\n  - Answer the question \"How can you identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset using Docta?\" with a credible, runnable-if-executed protocol.\n  - State assumptions (Docta identity/API, \"rare\" definition, synthetic-only evaluation) separately from fixed requirements \u2014 done here.\n  - No code execution, no invented outcome.\n\n=========================================================================\nEND OF DESIGN\n=========================================================================", "evidence": "Inspection confirms: /workspace contains only task.json; no repository, datasets (env datasets: []), or docs; filesystem search found no track.md on disk (only task.json's documents field referencing it as \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"); no package or file named \"docta\" anywhere; Python 3.14.4 with no pandas/numpy/sklearn and no mining libraries; network disabled. These constraints motivate every assumption: a functional contract for Docta (Section 1), synthetic ground-truth evaluation (Section 2), dual rarity criteria (Section 3), a parameter grid with adapters (Section 4), engine-agnostic metrics/controls (Section 5), and an analysis/feasibility plan with stated preconditions and pure-Python fallbacks, with no claim of execution (Sections 6-7).", "assumptions": ["Docta is an automated pattern-discovery/AutoML-style engine for tabular data exposing the stated functional contract; exact parameters/output unknown and bridged by thin adapters.", "'Rare pattern' = low support (tau 0.005-0.02) AND statistical interestingness (lift, chi-square/binomial p with FDR; WRAcc/relative-risk in supervised mode).", "Evaluation uses a purpose-built synthetic dataset with planted ground truth plus a noise-only negative control; no public dataset is available.", "The synthetic schema and six planted patterns are a reasonable reproducible proxy for a mixed tabular dataset.", "Future execution requires installing Docta or an equivalent shim plus baseline libraries (or documented pure-Python fallback bounds); this deliverable only specifies them."], "uncertainties": ["True identity/capabilities/API/parameter names of 'Docta' are undeterminable from the workspace (no repo/docs/package; network disabled); resolved by the explicit functional-contract assumption and adapter layer.", "Whether 'rare' is intended as frequency-based, interestingness-based, or rare-class outcome is not pinned; the design covers both label-free and supervised variants.", "Whether Docta natively supports rarity weighting, chi-square/WRAcc, and controlled max pattern length is unknown; each has a documented default/fallback mapping.", "Whether the evaluator intends real-interface verification; if not, the contract-based design remains the intended scope.", "Absolute runtime of a real Docta call is unknown; the scaling check and 96-point grid subsample hedge against this."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b08b6c00d91647cab8fb585690366c80", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full design-only experiment design using Docta for rare patterns", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
